  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 214 AM MDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nThe monsoon tap recovers somewhat on Tuesday into Wednesday, though \nthe flow will remain largely westerly aloft. Still, some low to mid \nlevel southerly flow over the Great Basin will help inject higher PW \ninto our area, pushing values back to around 0.75 and 1.2 inches or \nso across the area. This will of course be not quite as high as the \ncurrent monsoon surge, but should be sufficient to kick up coverage \nof showers and thunderstorms for Tuesday and Wednesday, especially \nwith some help from an upper level shortwave trough riding over the \ntop of the ridge Wednesday. While the boundary layer will still b on \nthe dry side during this period, there looks to be plenty of \nmoisture aloft so rain should still reach the ground even in the \ndrier areas. Tuesday should see temperatures climb just a smidge \nabove seasonal averages, while these values will fall by a few \ndegrees Wednesday with the aforementioned shortwave. \n\nThe upper level ridge will amplify to our west on Thursday, and \nFriday, turning the flow aloft to a more dry, northwesterly flow. \nExpect storm chances to become much more isolated once again as \ntemperatures climb back above seasonal averages. An early look at \n700-mb temperatures appears fairly marginal for record highs with \nthis round with NAEFS means hovering around +15 to +18C, or near the \nclimatological 90th percentile.",
